use in order to solve  analogies we're going to use computer  models of word meaning so computer  models of word meaning are built on the  fact that words that are similar are  used in similar ways so if we were to  look through all of the websites on the  internet and look for the word car and  the word truck they would probably also  both  be used in similar context they both be  used with the words gas drive door steer  etcetera and so that would tell us that  they have similar meaning and so what  computer models of weird meaning do is  to use that leverage that fact in order  to build models so we're going to  calculate statistics about the word  co-occurrence when two words occur  together and we're going to turn those  statistics into lists of numbers when  words have similar meaning they will  have similar lists so here I have I'm  representing the numbers with the color  blue and if it's more blue than it's a  larger number and so these two words car  and truck I've made a short list here  and because car and truck are similar  they have similar lists of numbers or  they're not exactly the same so we can  use this idea of lists of numbers in  order to tell if two words are similar  so hammer is two nail as screwdrivers to  screw now we need to use these lists of  numbers to actually perform the analogy  tasks so we're actually going to do is  literally do math on these lists of  numbers so we're gonna take the this  four nail and subtract the list four  hammer and add the list or screwdriver  just element by element and that gives  us a new vector it turns out that sorry  new list of numbers and that new list of  numbers tends to be close to the correct  answer to analogy task so this  hypothetical list of numbers is close to  the current statistics we see for the  word screw so let's test our computer  model we're going to do the analogy test  where we choose from five options and so  if we were a randomly guessing if you  didn't know what you were doing y