t on the left-hand side and  so now I'm the next slide I'm going to  show you essentially the results from  comparing a computer model of meaning to  a human brain and so here we're going to  be coloring the brain with these colors  that represent how correlated those  rankings are so if it's red or yellow  it's very correlated and if it's more of  a blue than it's less correlated and if  it's gray then it's not significant  there's no significant relationship  between the computer model and the brain  so here the first thing that notices  there's nothing that's gray which is  remarkable and interesting and not  something that was known before this  research was started we thought language  was really relegated to these one pieces  but it turns out that language meaning  is all over your brain you understand  language with your entire brain we also  see the same pattern of some more red  and yellow on the left hand side but we  see language meaning all over the brain  and this shows up in other studies too  so this is brain images captured while  people listen to podcasts and so here we  see if anything is black that mean  it's also not significantly related to  the computer model of meaning but here  we see lots of parts of the brain are  not black and that shows us that this  computer model of meaning is related to  how your brain registers meaning as  you're listening to a podcast so parts  of our brains do represent meaning in  the same way that computers do so  computers think like us are you  convinced I again I think you're right  to remain skeptical but it's interesting  to think about what it would take to  convince you that a computer understood  language thank you very much  [Applause]
 

